Course.start({"num":25,"unit":5,"slug":"m25-exponential","minutes":40,"calculator":"allowed","title":"Exponential Growth and Decay","subtitle":"Multiplying instead of adding: doubling, percent growth, depreciation and half-life","lede":"Linear things grow by adding the same amount. Exponential things grow by multiplying by the same amount, and that changes everything. This week: why a folded piece of paper could reach the Moon.","recap":"Exponent rules: multiply → add exponents, divide → subtract, power of a power → multiply. x⁰ = 1, x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ.","goals":["Tell linear and exponential patterns apart","Write and evaluate exponential functions y = a·bˣ","Model growth and decay with percents, y = a(1 + r)ᵗ and y = a(1 − r)ᵗ"],"context":{"title":"The student who folded paper 12 times","place":"Pomona, California · January 2002","text":"There's an old saying that you can't fold a piece of paper in half more than **7 or 8 times**, no matter how big it is. In **2001**, a high school junior named **Britney Gallivan** was offered extra credit to prove it wrong.\n\nBritney figured out *why* it's so hard. Every fold **doubles** the thickness: 1 layer, 2, 4, 8, 16, … After {{n}} folds there are {{2^n}} layers. But each fold also uses up more paper around the curved edge, so the paper has to be **much longer** than you'd think. She worked out the math, wrote an equation for the length needed, and then went to a mall in Pomona with a roll of special toilet paper about **4,000 feet** (1.2 km) long.\n\nIn January **2002**, after seven hours of careful folding with her family, Britney folded it in half **12 times**, a world record at the time. She was a high school student when she did it.\n\nThe math behind it is **exponential growth**. A sheet 0.1 mm thick, folded 42 times (if that were possible), would be {{0.1 * 2^42}} mm thick: more than **400,000 km**, farther than the Moon.","ask":"If each fold doubles the thickness, how many layers are there after 10 folds? (Hint: 2¹⁰.)"},"learn":[{"title":"Adding vs. multiplying","text":"- **Linear**: add (or subtract) the **same amount** each step. 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 (add 2). Graph: a straight line.\n- **Exponential**: multiply by the **same factor** each step. 3, 6, 12, 24, 48 (times 2). Graph: a curve that gets steeper and steeper.\n\nAt first they can look similar. But exponential growth always wins in the end: after 20 steps, adding 2 gets you to 43, while doubling gets you past **3 million**.","fig":"versus","check":{"q":"Is 5, 15, 45, 135, … linear or exponential?","choices":[["exponential: it multiplies by 3",true],["linear: it adds 10",false,"15 − 5 = 10, but 45 − 15 = 30. Not the same amount added."]],"keepOrder":true}},{"title":"Exponential functions","text":"An **exponential function** has the form\n\n> {{y = a * b^x}}\n\n- {{a}} is the **starting value** (when x = 0, {{b^0 = 1}}, so y = a).\n- {{b}} is the **growth factor**: what you multiply by each step.\n- If {{b > 1}}: **growth**. If {{0 < b < 1}}: **decay** (shrinking).","worked":[{"problem":"Bacteria start at 50 and double every hour. How many after 6 hours?","steps":[["{{y = 50 * 2^x}}","a = 50, b = 2."],["{{y = 50 * 2^6 = 50 * 64}}","Exponent first!"],["{{= 3200}} bacteria"]]},{"problem":"A 200 mg dose of medicine is cut in half every 4 hours (its **half-life**). How much is left after 12 hours?","steps":[["12 hours is 3 half-lives."],["{{200 * (1/2)^3 = 200 * 1/8}}"],["{{= 25}} mg"]]}]},{"title":"Percent growth and decay","text":"Growth or decay by a **percent** each year uses the same idea:\n\n- **Growth** by r percent: multiply by {{(1 + r)}}. Up 5% means ×1.05.\n- **Decay** by r percent: multiply by {{(1 - r)}}. Down 15% means ×0.85.\n\n> Growth: {{y = a(1 + r)^t}}    Decay: {{y = a(1 - r)^t}}\n\nA $20,000 car that loses 15% of its value each year is worth {{20000(0.85)^t}} after t years. After 3 years: {{20000(0.85)^3 ≈ 12283}} dollars.","tip":"Use a calculator's ^ key (or xʸ) for powers like 0.85³. Type the base, ^, then the exponent."}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"sort","title":"Linear or exponential?","cats":["Linear","Exponential","Neither"],"items":[["2, 4, 6, 8, …",0],["2, 4, 8, 16, …",1],["100, 50, 25, 12.5, …",1,"Multiplied by 1/2."],["100, 90, 80, 70, …",0],["{{y = 3x + 1}}",0],["{{y = 3^x}}",1],["{{y = 500(0.9)^t}}",1,"Decay: multiply by 0.9."],["1, 4, 9, 16, …",2,"Squares: neither adds nor multiplies by a constant."]],"hints":["Same difference → linear. Same ratio (divide each term by the one before) → exponential."]},{"id":"t2","type":"grid","title":"Linear vs. exponential table","intro":"Fill in both columns. Watch how fast the second one grows.","tables":[{"corner":"{{x}}","cols":["{{y = 5x}}","{{y = 2^x}}"],"rows":[["0","0","1"],["1","5","2"],["2","10","4"],["4","20","16"],["6","30","64"],["10","50","1024"]],"given":[[0,0],[0,1]]}],"hints":["{{2^6 = 2 * 2 * 2 * 2 * 2 * 2}}.","For y = 5x, just multiply by 5."]},{"id":"t3","type":"solve","title":"Evaluate","pick":5,"intro":"Calculator allowed. Exponent first, then multiply.","items":[{"q":"{{y = 3 * 2^x}} when {{x = 5}}","a":"96","from":"3*2^5","how":["{{2^5 = 32}}","{{3 * 32 = 96}}"]},{"q":"{{y = 100(1/2)^x}} when {{x = 3}}","a":"12.5","from":"100*(1/2)^3","how":["{{(1/2)^3 = 1/8}}","{{100/8 = 12.5}}"]},{"q":"{{f(x) = 4^x}}. Find {{f(3)}}.","a":"64","from":"4^3","how":["{{4^3 = 64}}"]},{"q":"{{y = 1000(1.1)^t}} when {{t = 2}}","a":"1210","from":"1000*1.1^2","how":["{{1.1^2 = 1.21}}","**1,210**"]},{"q":"{{y = 5 * 3^x}} when {{x = 0}}","a":"5","from":"5*3^0","how":["{{3^0 = 1}}: y = a = **5**"]},{"q":"{{y = 80(0.5)^x}} when {{x = 4}}","a":"5","from":"80*0.5^4","how":["{{0.5^4 = 0.0625}}","{{80 * 0.0625 = 5}}"]},{"q":"{{y = 2 * 3^x}} when {{x = -1}}","a":"2/3","from":"2*3^(-1)","how":["{{3^(-1) = 1/3}}","**2/3**"]}],"hints":["Do the power before multiplying: {{3 * 2^5}} is {{3 * 32}}, not {{6^5}}."]},{"id":"t4","type":"select","title":"Growth or decay?","items":["{{y = 50(1.08)^t}} shows {growth|decay} of {8%|108%|0.08%} per step.","{{y = 50(0.92)^t}} shows {decay|growth} of {8%|92%|0.92%} per step.","A population growing **3%** a year has growth factor {1.03|0.03|3|0.97}.","A car losing **20%** of its value each year has factor {0.8|1.2|0.2|20}.","In {{y = 6 * 2^x}}, the starting value is {6|2|12}."],"hints":["Growth factor = 1 + rate. Decay factor = 1 − rate.","Factor 1.08 → +8%. Factor 0.92 → −8%."]},{"id":"t5","type":"solve","title":"Real-world models","intro":"Calculator allowed. Write the model, then evaluate. Round money to the nearest cent and others as asked.","items":[{"q":"Britney's paper: each fold doubles the layers. How many layers after **12 folds**?","a":"4096","post":"layers","from":"2^12","how":["{{2^12 = 4096}}"]},{"q":"A video gets **200 views** on day 0, and the views **triple** each day. How many views on day 5?","a":"48600","post":"views","from":"200*3^5","how":["{{200 * 3^5 = 200 * 243}}","**48,600**"]},{"q":"**$500** is invested at **6%** interest per year. How much after **2 years**?","a":"561.8","pre":"$","from":"500*1.06^2","how":["{{500(1.06)^2 = 500 * 1.1236}}","**$561.80**"]},{"q":"A **$24,000** car loses **25%** of its value each year. What's it worth after **2 years**?","a":"13500","pre":"$","from":"24000*0.75^2","how":["{{24000(0.75)^2 = 24000 * 0.5625}}","**$13,500**"]},{"q":"A radioactive sample of **640 g** has a half-life of **5 years**. How many grams are left after **20 years**?","a":"40","post":"g","from":"640*(1/2)^4","how":["20 years = 4 half-lives","{{640 * (1/2)^4 = 640/16 = 40}} g"]}],"hints":["y = a · bᵗ: a is the start, b is the factor, t is the number of steps.","For half-life, count how many half-lives fit in the time."]},{"id":"t6","type":"quiz","title":"Read the graph","questions":[{"q":"Which graph shows exponential **growth**?","fig":"g3","choices":[["the curve that gets steeper (B)",true],["the straight line (A)",false,"A straight line is linear."],["the curve that flattens toward 0 (C)",false,"That one is decay."]]},{"q":"In curve B, {{y = 2^x}}. Where does it cross the y-axis?","choices":[["{{(0, 1)}}",true,"2⁰ = 1."],["{{(0, 0)}}",false,"2⁰ isn't 0."],["{{(0, 2)}}",false]]},{"q":"Does curve C, {{y = 8(1/2)^x}}, ever reach 0?","choices":[["No: it keeps halving, getting closer to 0 forever",true],["Yes, at x = 8",false],["Yes, at x = 3",false,"At x = 3 it's 1."]]}]}],"build":{"heading":"Linear or exponential?","prompt":"Someone offers you a choice for a 30-day job: **(A) $1,000 a day**, or **(B) 1 penny on day 1, 2 pennies on day 2, 4 on day 3**, doubling every day. Write a **model for each** (a linear one and an exponential one), calculate **day 30's pay** for each, and explain **which you'd choose and why**. (Calculator: day 30 of B is {{0.01 * 2^29}}.)","model":"Option A is linear: on day d it pays 1000 dollars, and the total after d days is 1000d. Option B is exponential: on day d it pays 0.01 × 2^(d - 1). On day 30, A pays $1,000, but B pays 0.01 × 2^29 = $5,368,709.12. I'd choose B because doubling grows so fast that by the end it pays millions, even though it starts with a penny.","placeholder":"Option A is linear: … Option B is exponential: …","reqs":[{"t":"Use the words *linear* and *exponential*","re":"\\b(linear|exponential)\\b","min":2},{"t":"An exponent in your model (^ or ²)","re":"\\^|[²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹]"},{"t":"Day 30's pay for option B (it's in the millions)","re":"5,?368,?709"},{"t":"Explain your choice (use *because*)","re":"\\bbecause\\b"}]},"reflect":{"q":"Britney Gallivan didn't just try harder: she figured out the math of *why* folding was hard, then used it to plan. When have you used understanding (not just effort) to solve a problem? How might that help you in math?","placeholder":"Once I figured out why…"},"challenge":[{"id":"x1","type":"solve","title":"Compound interest","intro":"Calculator. When interest is added {{n}} times a year: {{A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)}}.","items":[{"q":"**$1,000** at **6%**, compounded **monthly** ({{n = 12}}) for **1 year**. Round to the nearest cent.","a":"1061.68","pre":"$","how":["{{1000(1 + 0.06/12)^12 = 1000(1.005)^12}}","≈ **$1,061.68**"]},{"q":"How many **years** does it take money to **double** at 7% per year (compounded yearly)? Try values of t in {{(1.07)^t}}. Give the first whole number of years where it's at least double.","a":"11","post":"years","how":["{{1.07^10 ≈ 1.97}} and {{1.07^11 ≈ 2.10}}","**11 years** (the *rule of 72*: 72 ÷ 7 ≈ 10.3)"]}]},{"id":"x2","type":"solve","title":"Paper to the Moon","intro":"A sheet of paper is {{0.1}} mm thick. After {{n}} folds it would be {{0.1 * 2^n}} mm thick (if you could fold it).","items":[{"q":"How thick (in mm) after **20** folds?","a":"104857.6","post":"mm","from":"0.1*2^20","how":["{{0.1 * 1,048,576 = 104,857.6}} mm, about 105 meters: taller than a 30-story building!"]},{"q":"The Moon is about {{384,400}} km = {{3.844 * 10^11}} mm away. What's the **fewest** folds to reach it? Try {{0.1 * 2^n}} for n near 40.","a":"42","post":"folds","how":["{{0.1 * 2^41 ≈ 2.2 * 10^11}} mm: not enough.","{{0.1 * 2^42 ≈ 4.4 * 10^11}} mm: past the Moon. **42 folds**."]}]}],"figs":{"versus":{"x":[0,6],"y":[0,70],"ystep":10,"h":220,"w":280,"lines":[{"fn":"2x + 3","label":"add 2"},{"fn":"3*2^x","c":1,"label":"×2"}],"points":[[0,3],[1,5],[2,7],[3,9],[4,11],[5,13],[1,6,"",1],[2,12,"",1],[3,24,"",1],[4,48,"",1]],"alt":"A straight line adding 2 and a curve doubling, starting at 3"},"g3":{"x":[-1,4],"y":[-1,9],"w":240,"lines":[{"fn":"x + 1","label":"A"},{"fn":"2^x","c":1,"label":"B"},{"fn":"8*(1/2)^x","c":2,"label":"C","at":[3,1]}],"alt":"Graph A is a line, B is a curve rising steeply, C is a curve falling toward 0"}},"culture":"**Did you know?** After Britney's record, students at St. Mark's School in Massachusetts folded a 16-kilometer roll of toilet paper 13 times in 2011, along MIT's Infinite Corridor. Doubling makes each new fold dramatically harder.","unitTitle":"Systems and Exponents","code":"MjAwMi1HTklETE9G","star":"NTkxNi1TSUdBTQ==","total":30,"prev":"m24-exponent-rules.html","next":"m26-polynomials.html"});
